  {
   "id": "xgLsOW5nAwAJ",
   "from": "Brendan",
   "author": "7d2a96af532ed7de",
   "date": "2015-10-12T22:18:05Z",
   "subject": "Quantile Regression",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear List,<div><br></div><div>Quantile regression has come up <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/stan-users/%22quantile$20regression%22/stan-users/DUv_uFFedCM/Y5KgALJqbP4J\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!searchin/stan-users/%2522quantile$20regression%2522/stan-users/DUv_uFFedCM/Y5KgALJqbP4J&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485568000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3OMmd_fSznAJLI10NOXuSb\">once</a> or <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/stan-users/%22quantile$20regression%22/stan-users/uvj_WNQQM_Y/NgSvxSbmCAAJ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!searchin/stan-users/%2522quantile$20regression%2522/stan-users/uvj_WNQQM_Y/NgSvxSbmCAAJ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485568000&amp;usg=AOvVaw14PrqJ-smMkLip8uBn2aoM\">twice</a> before, but I can&#39;t seem to find a reproducible example of anyone who&#39;s got it working.</div><div><br></div><div>The only MCMC code I can seem to find online is <a href=\"http://stats.stackexchange.com/a/17717\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stats.stackexchange.com/a/17717&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485568000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3-ss50pRAFumhB7K2KxOJ2\">this example</a> from a JAGS model, based on a data augmentation procedure described by Reed &amp; Yu (2009 )[1].</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve converted it to Stan code (.Rmd file attached, and on GitHub, <a href=\"https://github.com/brendan-R/quantile_regression/blob/master/quantile_regression.Rmd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/brendan-R/quantile_regression/blob/master/quantile_regression.Rmd&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485568000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3c7LbwFasLDYF5Tvkhc03a\">here</a>, html output <a href=\"https://rawgit.com/brendan-r/quantile_regression/master/quantile_regression.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://rawgit.com/brendan-r/quantile_regression/master/quantile_regression.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485568000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2Yl8qD1lbH1Sg4eNJrY097\">here</a>), and compared the results to the JAGS output, and results from the bayesQR and quantreg R packages.</div><div><br></div><div>Unfortunately I&#39;m getting whacky results out of Stan. I can&#39;t seem to get the model to converge, and the coefficients look pretty far off. My questions:</div><div><br></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 40px;border:none;padding:0px\"><div>1. Can anyone spot a problem in my Stan code? Some of the warning messages are unexpected. For example, I&#39;m getting warnings that the exponential distribution function is being fed negative values, which shouldn&#39;t be possible (tau is constrained to be positive, as far as I&#39;ve understood my own code).</div><div><br></div><div>2. Could it be that this particular algorithm is only suitable for Gibbs sampling? (From skimming the paper, it seems like this may be the case, however, I&#39;m no expert!)</div><div><br></div><div>3. If this quantile regression algorithm is peculiar to GIbbs sampling, what procedure, if any, might be appropriate for Stan?</div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m keen to use Stan (as opposed to falling back to JAGS/Gibbs), as the final model I&#39;d like to fit is QR on-top-of a large hierarchical model.<br></div><div><br></div><div>A minimal reproducible example of quantile regression would be an excellent addition to the Stan list/manual!</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div><br></div><div>Brendan</div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;line-height:16.12px\">Reed, C., &amp; Yu, K. (2009). A Partially collapsed Gibbs sampler for Bayesian quantile regression. </span></div><div><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;line-height:16.12px\">URL: </span><font face=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"line-height:16.12px\"><a href=\"http://bura.brunel.ac.uk/bitstream/2438/3593/1/fulltext.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://bura.brunel.ac.uk/bitstream/2438/3593/1/fulltext.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485568000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3Mv9XNIYDftJoM1KEalumC\">http://bura.brunel.ac.uk/bitstream/2438/3593/1/fulltext.pdf</a></span></font></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "bcaC1yV6AwAJ",
   "from": "Brendan",
   "author": "7d2a96af532ed7de",
   "date": "2015-10-13T04:01:05Z",
   "subject": "Re: Quantile Regression",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">In case Rmarkdown is a bit fiddly, here are the model specs:<br><br>JAGS:<div><pre style=\"box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:13.6px;font-stretch:normal;line-height:1.45;padding:16px;border-radius:3px;word-wrap:normal;word-break:normal;color:rgb(51,51,51);background-color:rgb(247,247,247)\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  model{</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">    for(i in 1:length(y)){</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">      mu[i] &lt;- alpha + beta * x[i]</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">      w[i]  ~ dexp(tau)</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">      me[i] &lt;- (1 - 2 * p) / (p * (1 - p)) * w[i] + mu[i]</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">      pe[i] &lt;- (p * (1 - p) * tau) / (2 * w[i])</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">      y[i]  ~ dnorm(me[i], pe[i])</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">    }</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\"></span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">    # Regression Priors</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">    alpha ~ dnorm(0, 1E-6)</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">    beta  ~ dnorm(0, 1E-6)</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\"></span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">    lsigma ~ dunif(-5, 15)</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">    sigma  &lt;- exp(lsigma / 2)</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">    tau    &lt;- pow(sigma, -2)</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  }</span></pre></div><div><br></div><div>Stan:</div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;font-family:Consolas,&#39;Liberation Mono&#39;,Menlo,Courier,monospace;font-size:13.6px;font-stretch:normal;line-height:1.45;padding:16px;border-radius:3px;word-wrap:normal;word-break:normal;color:rgb(51,51,51);background-color:rgb(247,247,247)\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">data {</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  int&lt;lower=0&gt; N;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  real&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; p;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  vector[N] x;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  vector[N] y;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">}</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">parameters {</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  real alpha;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  real beta;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  real&lt;lower=0.001, upper=10&gt; sigma;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  vector[N] w;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">}</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">transformed parameters {</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  real&lt;lower=0&gt; tau;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  vector[N] mu;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  vector[N] me;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  vector[N] pe;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\"></span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  tau &lt;- pow(sigma, -2);</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  mu  &lt;- alpha + beta * x;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  me  &lt;- (1 - 2 * p) / (p * (1 - p)) * w + mu;</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  pe  &lt;- (p * (1 - p) * tau) ./ (2 * w);</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">}</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">model {</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  # Priors</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  alpha ~ normal(0, 100);</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  beta  ~ normal(0, 100);</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  sigma ~ cauchy(0, 2.5);</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\"></span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  # Data Augmentation</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  w     ~ exponential(tau);</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\"></span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  # The model</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">  y     ~ normal(me, pe);</span>\r\n<span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(24,54,145)\">}</span></pre></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Monday, 12 October 2015 15:18:05 UTC-7, Brendan  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Dear List,<div><br></div><div>Quantile regression has come up <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/stan-users/%22quantile$20regression%22/stan-users/DUv_uFFedCM/Y5KgALJqbP4J\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!searchin/stan-users/%2522quantile$20regression%2522/stan-users/DUv_uFFedCM/Y5KgALJqbP4J&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485575000&amp;usg=AOvVaw12XL1hugedSaEPXApVdhXn\">once</a> or <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/stan-users/%22quantile$20regression%22/stan-users/uvj_WNQQM_Y/NgSvxSbmCAAJ\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!searchin/stan-users/%2522quantile$20regression%2522/stan-users/uvj_WNQQM_Y/NgSvxSbmCAAJ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485575000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3ULSdCTdsvbwKYDozPPOfl\">twice</a> before, but I can&#39;t seem to find a reproducible example of anyone who&#39;s got it working.</div><div><br></div><div>The only MCMC code I can seem to find online is <a href=\"http://stats.stackexchange.com/a/17717\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stats.stackexchange.com/a/17717&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485575000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0K9CEyqVZIlpjjx6xjr53w\">this example</a> from a JAGS model, based on a data augmentation procedure described by Reed &amp; Yu (2009 )[1].</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ve converted it to Stan code (.Rmd file attached, and on GitHub, <a href=\"https://github.com/brendan-R/quantile_regression/blob/master/quantile_regression.Rmd\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/brendan-R/quantile_regression/blob/master/quantile_regression.Rmd&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485575000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1W7KS-UK5V1mLVu8w37---\">here</a>, html output <a href=\"https://rawgit.com/brendan-r/quantile_regression/master/quantile_regression.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://rawgit.com/brendan-r/quantile_regression/master/quantile_regression.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485575000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1KOyGEcZ72ZGunm9VRnVHi\">here</a>), and compared the results to the JAGS output, and results from the bayesQR and quantreg R packages.</div><div><br></div><div>Unfortunately I&#39;m getting whacky results out of Stan. I can&#39;t seem to get the model to converge, and the coefficients look pretty far off. My questions:</div><div><br></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 40px;border:none;padding:0px\"><div>1. Can anyone spot a problem in my Stan code? Some of the warning messages are unexpected. For example, I&#39;m getting warnings that the exponential distribution function is being fed negative values, which shouldn&#39;t be possible (tau is constrained to be positive, as far as I&#39;ve understood my own code).</div><div><br></div><div>2. Could it be that this particular algorithm is only suitable for Gibbs sampling? (From skimming the paper, it seems like this may be the case, however, I&#39;m no expert!)</div><div><br></div><div>3. If this quantile regression algorithm is peculiar to GIbbs sampling, what procedure, if any, might be appropriate for Stan?</div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m keen to use Stan (as opposed to falling back to JAGS/Gibbs), as the final model I&#39;d like to fit is QR on-top-of a large hierarchical model.<br></div><div><br></div><div>A minimal reproducible example of quantile regression would be an excellent addition to the Stan list/manual!</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div><br></div><div>Brendan</div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;line-height:16.12px\">Reed, C., &amp; Yu, K. (2009). A Partially collapsed Gibbs sampler for Bayesian quantile regression. </span></div><div><span style=\"font-family:Arial,sans-serif;line-height:16.12px\">URL: </span><font face=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"line-height:16.12px\"><a href=\"http://bura.brunel.ac.uk/bitstream/2438/3593/1/fulltext.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://bura.brunel.ac.uk/bitstream/2438/3593/1/fulltext.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463485575000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2uOgg_UnosbzSNv0MmLzmJ\">http://bura.brunel.ac.uk/bitstream/2438/3593/1/fulltext.pdf</a></span></font></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
